1. TerraSentia

🔗 ￼https://www.earthsense.co/terrasentia

Description

TerraSentia is a small autonomous agricultural robot designed to navigate between crop rows and collect detailed plant data. It focuses on under-canopy monitoring, allowing it to capture information that aerial systems like drones cannot access.  ￼

Methods Used
	•	Computer vision for plant analysis
	•	Machine learning for crop measurements
	•	Cameras and sensors
	•	Vision-based navigation

Design Approach

The robot is lightweight and compact, designed to move between narrow crop rows. It uses a dual-system structure where high-level processing handles analysis and low-level systems control movement.
Implementation Approach

The robot moves through farms collecting plant data and analyzing crop conditions such as growth and disease detection in real time.  ￼



2. Bonirob

🔗 ￼https://www.researchgate.net/publication/375466023_BoniRob_an_autonomous_field_robot_platform_for_individual_plant_phenotyping

Description

Bonirob is an autonomous agricultural robot used for crop monitoring, weed detection, and field research. It is widely used in precision agriculture.  ￼

Methods Used
	•	Computer vision
	•	Machine learning
	•	GPS navigation
	•	Sensor-based monitoring

Design Approach

Bonirob uses a modular design, meaning tools and sensors can be changed depending on the task. This makes it flexible for different farming operations.  ￼

Implementation Approach

It operates in farms by collecting crop data, identifying weeds, and performing targeted actions such as mechanical weeding instead of using chemicals.  ￼



